"direction": "significant", "effect_strength": "strong", "hedging": false, "claim": "The sector classification meaningfully explained variation in investment responses."}], "primary_direction": "significant", "has_cross_section_conflict": false, "any_hedging": false, "sections": ["results"]}, {"test_type": "t", "statistic_value": -4.19, "df1": 92.0, "df2": null, "reported_p": 0.01, "p_equality": "=", "two_tailed": false, "raw_text": "t(92) = −4.19, p = 0.01", "textual_interpretation": "significant", "interpretation_direction": "significant", "consistent": true, "notes": "one-tailed independent samples t-test comparing SMEs and large corporations on working capital sensitivity", "interpretations": [{"section": "results", "sentence": "Subsequently, we conducted a focused comparison between small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) and large corporations regarding the sensitivity of working capital management to liquidity constraints; a one-tailed independent samples t-test with 92 degrees of freedom yielded t(92) = −4.19, p = 0.01, demonstrating that SMEs exhibited significantly lower working capital flexibility compared to large firms, as hypothesized.", "keywords": ["significantly lower", "as hypothesized"], "direction": "significant", "effect_strength": "strong", "hedging": false, "claim": "SMEs exhibited significantly lower working capital flexibility compared to large firms."}, {"section": "results", "sentence": "This directional finding supported our theoretical prediction that financial frictions disproportionately constrain smaller firms' operational efficiency.", "keywords": ["supported our theoretical prediction", "disproportionately constrain"], "direction": "significant", "effect_strength": "strong", "hedging": false, "claim": "The findings support that financial frictions disproportionately constrain smaller firms' operational efficiency."}], "primary_direction": "significant", "has_cross_section_conflict": false, "any_hedging": false, "sections": ["resul