 and self-reported emotional exhaustion scores was negligible and not statistically significant."}], "primary_direction": "not_significant", "has_cross_section_conflict": false, "any_hedging": false, "sections": ["results"]}], "verified_tests": [{"test_type": "chi", "statistic_value": 18.32, "df1": 9.0, "df2": null, "reported_p": 0.168, "p_equality": "=", "two_tailed": true, "raw_text": "χ²(9) = 18.32, p = 0.168", "textual_interpretation": "no significant association between occupational type and primary coping strategy selection", "interpretation_direction": "not_significant", "consistent": true, "notes": "chi-square test of independence for coping strategy preference across occupational categories", "interpretations": [{"section": "results", "sentence": "The sample consisted of 144 employed adults (Mage = 38.7 years, SD = 11.2; 58% female) recruited from three organizational sectors. We first examined whether coping strategy preference varied significantly across occupational categories using a chi-square test of independence, which revealed no significant association between occupational type and primary coping strategy selection, χ²(9) = 18.32, p = 0.168, indicating that employees across different occupational domains did not significantly differ in their preferred stress management approaches.", "keywords": ["no significant association", "did not significantly differ"], "direction": "not_significant", "effect_strength": "none", "hedging": false, "claim": "There was no significant association between occupational type and primary coping strategy selection."}], "primary_direction": "not_significant", "has_cross_section_conflict": false, "any_hedging": false, "sections": ["results"], "computed_p": 0.0316373334, "p_consistency": "inconsistent", "interpretation_consistency": "inconsistent"}, {"test_type": "r", "statistic_value": -0.07, "df1": 142.0, "df2": null, "reported_p": 0.43, "p_equality": "=", "two_tailed": true, "raw_text": "r(142) = -0.07, p = 0.43", "textu